<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gregory the Illuminator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 257–c. 331</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Gregory the Illuminator, Grigor Lusavorich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory the Illuminator was the founder and first head of the Armenian Church. According to tradition he was imprisoned for years in a pit at Khor Virap by King Tiridates III, then healed and converted the king, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as its state religion in the early 4th century, traditionally in 301. Consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> at Caesarea, he organised the church, and his descendants held its leadership for generations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator.jpg" alt="Gregory the Illuminator" width="320" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G.dallorto · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_the_Illuminator_mosaic_on_Pammakaristos_Church_in_Constantinople.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
